<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="credentials"
      updated="August 21, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"which-one", "Which credential is this?"},
        {"sequence", "The rotation sequence"},
        {"mcp-keys", "Agent keys"},
        {"audit-tokens", "Audit-export tokens"},
        {"idp", "OIDC client secrets and SCIM bearers"},
        {"signing", "Signed-dispatch keys"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="credentials">
        <:dek>
          emisar issues several kinds of secret, and they do not behave the same way. Find yours,
          rotate it without an avoidable outage, and prove the old one no longer carries the
          authority you thought it did.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Operators can manage their own agent keys. Owners and admins can manage any agent key in the
          account.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Access to whatever holds the current value — the client config, the SIEM, the provisioning template, your IdP.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          A window in which you can watch the consumer. Every rotation below ends with proving the
          new path works, not with pasting a value.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="which-one">Which credential is this?</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The prefix alone will not identify the credential. Both agent keys and audit-export tokens use the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emk-</code>
        prefix. Their kinds are different and do not overlap. Start here, then follow your
        row's owner page — the runner-held credentials live on <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-credentials"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runner credentials</.link>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[1280px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Credential</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Where it is used and stored</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Authority</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Routine rotation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Immediate containment</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Overlap</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Effect on sessions and runners</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Owner page</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <%= for {credential, where, authority, rotation, containment, overlap, effect, {owner_path, owner}} <- [
              {"Agent key",
               "A local client or bridge config on a workstation. OAuth clients hold access and refresh tokens instead.",
               "Calls MCP tools with its owner's runner scope and role",
               "Rotate the key — the successor inherits its name, kind, and scope",
               "Revoke — terminal, and it takes the rotation chain with it",
               "Yes — until the successor's first successful call",
               "No sign-in ends. Revocation also stops OAuth access and refresh tokens backed by that key.",
               {~p"/docs/agents-and-keys", "Manage agents & keys"}},
              {"Audit-export token",
               "Your SIEM or poller config",
               "Reads GET /api/audit for this account, and nothing else",
               "Mint a second token, move the collector to it, revoke the first",
               "Revoke",
               "Yes, but you create it — two live tokens until you revoke",
               "None — it has no session and reaches no runner",
               {~p"/docs/audit-and-siem", "Audit & SIEM"}},
              {"Runner enrollment key",
               "The provisioning template that sets EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY, and /etc/emisar/runner.env on the host",
               "Registers new runners into the account",
               "Mint new, update the template, boot one host, revoke old",
               "Revoke — stops new registrations only",
               "Yes — both keys register until you revoke the old one",
               "Connected runners are unaffected. They use their own tokens.",
               {~p"/docs/runner-credentials", "Runner credentials"}},
              {"Per-runner token",
               "Cached on the host by the runner, issued at registration",
               "That one runner's control connection",
               "Automatic — the runner swaps itself onto a successor at 60 days",
               "Delete the runner — terminal. Disable is the reversible lever",
               "Yes — the outgoing token works for 24 hours after the swap",
               "That runner disconnects. If disabled, the service keeps retrying. If deleted, it exits.",
               {~p"/docs/runner-credentials", "Runner credentials"}},
              {"OIDC client secret",
               "The SSO connection here, and the app registration at your IdP",
               "Authenticates emisar to your identity provider during sign-in",
               "Create at the IdP, save here, complete a real sign-in, drop the old",
               "Disable or delete the connection",
               "Yes, if your IdP can hold two client secrets at once",
               "Replacing the secret ends no session. Disabling or deleting the connection revokes sessions authenticated through it.",
               {~p"/docs/sso", "Single sign-on"}},
              {"SCIM bearer",
               "Your IdP's provisioning connector",
               "Directory reads and writes on that connection",
               "Rotate token — it takes effect immediately",
               "Rotate token, or disable the connection",
               "None — provisioning fails until the IdP holds the new value",
               "No session ends. Provisioning stops until the connector is updated.",
               {~p"/docs/scim", "Directory sync"}},
              {"Signed-dispatch leaf",
               "The operator's machine, in the MCP bridge process, as the signing key and its certificate",
               "Signs an action a runner that trusts the CA will accept",
               "Mint a new certificate under the CA the fleet already trusts",
               "None per leaf — rotate the CA across the fleet",
               "Yes — every unexpired certificate under a trusted CA keeps working",
               "No runner is touched and no run is dropped",
               {~p"/docs/signed-dispatch", "Signed dispatch"}},
              {"Signed-dispatch CA",
               "The trusted_cas list on every runner. The CA private key stays offline.",
               "Vouches for every leaf a runner will accept",
               "Add the new CA, reissue leaves, remove the old, reload every runner",
               "The same operation, done urgently",
               "Yes — while both CAs are in the list",
               "Each step applies on reload without dropping a run. A missed host still trusts the old CA.",
               {~p"/docs/signed-dispatch", "Signed dispatch"}},
              {"Pack or provider credential",
               "/etc/emisar/runner.env on the host, read at process start",
               "Whatever the provider grants that value. The control plane does not distribute or store it as configuration.",
               "Rotate at the provider, update runner.env, restart the service",
               "Revoke at the provider",
               "The provider's own, if it issues overlapping credentials",
               "The restart interrupts actions in flight on that host. Nothing else changes.",
               {~p"/docs/runner-credentials", "Runner credentials"}}
            ] do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">{credential}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{where}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{authority}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{rotation}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{containment}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{overlap}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{effect}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                  <.link navigate={owner_path} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
                    {owner}
                  </.link>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="sequence">The rotation sequence</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each credential below follows the same seven steps. If a credential has no overlap window,
        its section names the expected interruption.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300 list-decimal pl-6">
        <li>Inventory: find every consumer that holds the current value.</li>
        <li>Create the successor.</li>
        <li>Update the consumer.</li>
        <li>Verify the new path — a real call, a real sign-in, a real registration.</li>
        <li>Revoke the predecessor.</li>
        <li>Verify the old value now fails.</li>
        <li>Read the audit trail: the mint, the revoke, and the actor behind each.</li>
      </ol>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Never paste a secret value into anything that keeps it.">
        <p>
          Never use a support ticket, run reason, action argument, or copied shell example. A
          credential appears once at creation. If the consumer misses it, create another. Do not
          store it in the wrong place.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="mcp-keys">Agent keys</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Rotate or revoke one from the agent's row on <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Agents</a>. Rotation is safe to do at any time: the predecessor keeps authenticating until
        the successor's first call retires it, so the client can pick up the new secret whenever you
        get to it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Revocation is the one with reach. It is terminal, and it takes every downstream key in the
        rotation chain plus any OAuth token backed by that key. That is why revoke, not rotate, is
        the containment lever for a leak — and why an OAuth-backed connection is replaced by
        reconnecting the client rather than rotated.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The procedures, the scope a key inherits, expiry, and the bridge's own rotation of a
        nearly-expired key are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Manage agents & keys</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="audit-tokens">Audit-export tokens</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An audit-export token shares the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emk-</code>
        prefix with an agent key, but it is a different credential kind. It reads only your account
        events. The MCP endpoint rejects an audit-export token as the wrong kind. The export API
        rejects an agent key the same way. This separation prevents a SIEM credential from dispatching actions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        There is no successor mechanism, so create the overlap. Mint a second token from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/audit/export"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Audit export</a>
        and give it to the collector. Keep the saved cursor. The cursor belongs to the collector,
        not the token. The new credential resumes from the cursor left by the old token. Let one
        poll finish and save its returned cursor.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This test proves token authentication and continued cursor storage. Cutovers often fail at
        that point. The API does not guarantee exactly-once delivery. A collector can re-read or
        repeat events if it restarts from
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">since</code>
        or crashes after processing but before saving the cursor. Saving the cursor before the sink
        accepts the page can skip events. Revoke the old token and confirm it returns <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>.
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Audit &amp; SIEM</.link>
        documents cursors, paging, and rate limits.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="idp">OIDC client secrets and SCIM bearers</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Both live on an SSO connection, and they fail in opposite directions — one has an overlap,
        the other has none.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">OIDC client secret.</strong>
            Create the replacement at your IdP, paste it into the connection, and save. The field is
            write-only. Leaving it blank keeps the existing value. Complete a real sign-in through
            the saved connection. Then delete the old secret at the IdP. A connection test proves
            only discovery and reachability. It does not prove client assignment or login policy.
            Replacing the secret signs no one out.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM bearer.</strong>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotate token</strong>
            replaces the bearer immediately with no overlap. The old bearer stops authenticating at
            rotation. Your IdP connector fails until you enter the new value. Plan for this
            provisioning gap. Existing signed-in users stay online.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Disabling or deleting a connection is a different blast radius.</strong>
            It blocks new sign-ins and revokes sessions authenticated through identities on that
            connection. It does not touch API keys or OAuth credentials, and it does not suspend or
            delete members. Disabling SCIM also returns role and runner access to owner and admin
            control and discards the last group snapshot.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The provider-side steps are in
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Single sign-on</.link>
        and <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Directory sync</.link>.
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Authentication</.link>
        covers complete offboarding.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="signing">Signed-dispatch keys</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Rotating an operator signing key touches no runner. Mint a new leaf and certificate under
        the fleet trusted CA. Switch the MCP client to it. Set the certificate lifetime above the
        freshness window plus approval turnaround. This keeps the original signature inside both
        windows when an approved run is re-dispatched.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="There is no per-certificate kill switch.">
        <p>
          emisar publishes no revocation list for signing certificates. A leaked leaf key works
          until its certificate expires. Short lifetimes limit exposure. For earlier containment,
          rotate the trust root. Add the new CA beside the old CA. Reissue operator certificates
          under the new CA. Remove the old CA and reload every runner. Every runner must reload.
          The control plane cannot do this for you.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The CA model, certificate scopes, and the reload mechanics are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Signed dispatch</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
